# FAIL-CLOSED DEPLOY SURFACE — this is an ALLOW-LIST, not a deny-list.
#
# The first rule excludes EVERYTHING. Each "!" line then re-includes exactly one
# approved public file. Anything new — a script, a cache, a corpus, a log — is
# therefore excluded BY DEFAULT and can never reach the public surface merely
# because nobody remembered to add a deny pattern. The previous deny-list was
# fail-open and silently published every unlisted script.
#
# vercel.json is uploaded because Vercel reads it as routing/header configuration;
# Vercel consumes it and never serves it, so it must answer 404 in production.
#
# verify-deploy-surface.js (npm run verify:surface) enforces this exact shape,
# proves a hypothetical new file is excluded, and asserts the live surface on both
# production domains. Editing this file without updating that verifier fails the run.
*
!index.html
!app.js
!styles.css
!signals.json
!predictions.json
!author.json
!vercel.json
!LICENSE

# Belt-and-braces: the highest-risk names are also denied explicitly and last, so
# they win over any future re-include. "*" already excludes them.
.env*
.vercel
.pipeline.lock